import {makeCards} from './helpers';

const g=(leaf,rows)=>makeCards(['Schools of Economic Thought','Schools & Thinkers',leaf],rows);

export const schoolsEarly=[
...g('Mercantilism & the Physiocrats',[
['Mercantilism','Why did early modern states often treat trade surpluses as instruments of national power?','Mercantilist policy linked commerce to state capacity. Export surpluses, shipping, colonial monopolies and bullion were valued because they could support taxation, war finance and strategic industries. The mistake is to read mercantilism simply as a theory that exports are good and imports bad: it was also a political economy of rivalry among states operating before modern central banking and national accounting.'],
['Bullionism','Why did stocks of gold and silver appear economically decisive to some early modern governments?','Bullionism identified monetary metals with national strength because specie settled international payments and financed armies and state obligations. In economies with limited credit systems, gold and silver constraints were real. Classical economists later attacked the inference that accumulating bullion itself constituted wealth, distinguishing money from the productive resources and goods that determine real income.'],
['Trade Surpluses','Why can a trade surplus strengthen one objective without being a general measure of national welfare?','A surplus means exports exceed imports in the current account component of trade, but it is matched by corresponding financial flows. Mercantilists valued surpluses partly for strategic and monetary reasons. Modern economics asks what drives saving, investment, exchange rates and capital flows instead. A surplus can reflect competitiveness, weak domestic demand, high saving or demographic structure; its welfare meaning is therefore not fixed.'],
['Navigation Acts','What economic problem were navigation laws trying to solve from a state-power perspective?','Navigation laws restricted shipping and trade to favored vessels or routes in order to build domestic maritime capacity and weaken rivals. They could foster strategic shipping industries, but they also raised trade costs and protected incumbent merchants. Their logic is easier to understand as strategic state-building than as ordinary consumer-welfare maximization.'],
['Chartered Monopolies','Why did governments grant monopoly trading rights to private companies?','Chartered companies bundled commerce, finance, administration and sometimes military power. Exclusive rights helped firms bear high fixed costs and political risks in long-distance trade, while states gained revenue and geopolitical reach. The same privileges also created monopoly rents and opportunities for coercion, showing how early commercial expansion mixed private enterprise with delegated state power.'],
['Colbertism','How did Colbertism differ from simple protectionism?','Jean-Baptiste Colbert pursued a coordinated program of manufacturing promotion, quality regulation, infrastructure, tariffs and state support under Louis XIV. The aim was not merely to block imports but to build productive and fiscal capacity. Colbertism illustrates a broader mercantilist belief that the structure of production could be deliberately shaped for national power.'],
['Strategic Trade Before Modern Theory','Why can a policy that seems inefficient in a competitive model look different under strategic rivalry?','If industries have scale economies, learning effects or military importance, a government may value domestic capacity beyond immediate consumer surplus. Early mercantilists often reasoned this way implicitly. Modern strategic-trade theory formalizes narrower cases, but it also shows the danger: governments need unusually good information to identify rents rather than simply subsidizing politically connected firms.'],
['Import Substitution in Early Modern Policy','Why did states try to produce at home goods they previously imported?','Reducing import dependence could conserve specie, develop domestic skills and improve wartime resilience. Yet forced substitution can raise costs when local producers lack comparative advantage. The key distinction is between temporary capability building with credible learning effects and permanent protection that shelters inefficient producers.'],
['Colonial Trade Monopolies','How did colonial monopoly rules redistribute gains from trade?','Metropolitan powers often required colonies to trade through designated ports, firms or shipping systems. These rules redirected rents toward favored merchants and the imperial state while restricting colonial producers and consumers. The resulting trade patterns were political allocations, not simply expressions of comparative advantage.'],
['Specie-Flow Mechanism','Why does a persistent trade surplus become difficult to sustain under a metallic monetary system?','David Hume’s price-specie-flow mechanism showed that surplus countries receive specie, expanding domestic money and prices, while deficit countries lose specie and experience the reverse. Relative-price changes then tend to reduce the original imbalance. The argument undermined the idea that every country could continuously accumulate bullion through trade policy.'],
['Physiocracy','Why did the Physiocrats identify agriculture as uniquely productive?','Physiocrats argued that agriculture generated a net product above the costs required to reproduce inputs, while manufacturing merely transformed existing value. That substantive claim did not survive later economics, but the school was important for thinking systematically about surplus, circular flows and the economy as an interdependent system.'],
['François Quesnay','What was analytically important about Quesnay even though his agricultural theory was wrong?','Quesnay’s Tableau économique represented flows of output and income among social classes. It anticipated later input-output and national-accounting thinking by asking how one period’s production finances and reproduces the next. His contribution was therefore partly methodological: the economy could be represented as a connected circulation rather than a set of isolated markets.'],
['Tableau Économique','What problem was the Tableau Économique trying to solve?','The Tableau traced how the agricultural surplus circulated among farmers, landowners and the so-called sterile class of manufacturers. Quesnay wanted to show the conditions under which the economy reproduces itself. Although the categories are obsolete, the idea that expenditure flows must connect sectoral incomes and outputs is foundational to later macroeconomic accounting.'],
['Produit Net','What did the Physiocrats mean by a net product?','Produit net was the surplus left after replacing the inputs required for production. Physiocrats believed only agriculture generated this surplus because nature augmented agricultural output. Modern economics rejects that sectoral restriction, but the broader concept of an economic surplus after reproduction costs influenced classical theories of distribution.'],
['The Single Tax','Why did Physiocrats favor taxing land rent rather than many separate activities?','If land alone generated the net surplus, they reasoned that ultimately all taxes fell on that surplus anyway. A single land tax would therefore simplify the system and reduce distortions. Modern land-value-tax arguments use different theory but share the idea that taxing an inelastic land base can generate revenue with relatively low efficiency cost.'],
['Laissez-Faire Origins','Why did laissez-faire first emerge as a critique of detailed economic regulation?','Physiocrats opposed internal tolls, grain restrictions and many guild-like controls because they believed a natural economic order would function better with freer exchange. Their laissez-faire was not an argument for the absence of legal institutions; property and state authority still underpinned the order they wanted markets to operate within.'],
['Circular Flow','Why is circular-flow reasoning more profound than the diagram usually taught in introductory economics?','Circular-flow analysis links one agent’s spending to another’s income and one sector’s output to another’s input. It imposes accounting consistency on the economy as a whole. Quesnay used an early version of this logic; modern national accounts, input-output tables and stock-flow models build far more sophisticated versions of the same idea.'],
['Agricultural Surplus','Why was agricultural surplus central to preindustrial political economy?','In predominantly agrarian economies, the amount of output remaining after subsistence and seed requirements constrained urbanization, state revenue and specialization. Even though later economists rejected the Physiocratic claim that only agriculture is productive, the size and distribution of agricultural surplus genuinely mattered for preindustrial development.'],
['Internal Free Trade','Why could removing internal trade barriers be as important as changing international tariffs?','Early modern economies often contained local tolls, monopolies and fragmented rules that raised the cost of moving goods inside the country. Removing these barriers expanded effective market size, encouraged specialization and improved price integration. This institutional dimension of market formation later became important in Smithian growth analysis.'],
['Mercantilism vs Physiocracy','What was the deepest disagreement between mercantilists and Physiocrats?','Mercantilist thought emphasized commerce, manufacturing and state power, whereas Physiocrats located the fundamental surplus in agriculture and favored freer internal economic organization. The disagreement reflected different theories of wealth and production. Both nevertheless treated the economy as embedded in a political order rather than as an abstract collection of utility-maximizing individuals.']
]),
...g('Adam Smith & Classical Economics',[
['Adam Smith','Why is Smith poorly summarized as “self-interest plus the invisible hand”?','Smith analyzed specialization, market expansion, capital accumulation, competition, institutions and moral norms as parts of one system. Self-interest can coordinate exchange under competition, but the same motive can produce collusion or privilege when institutions permit it. Smith also assigned government important roles in justice, defense, education and public works.'],
['The Wealth of Nations','What problem is The Wealth of Nations fundamentally trying to explain?','Smith asks what causes the annual produce of a society to grow and how that produce is distributed. His answer combines specialization, capital accumulation, market size, institutions and competition. The book is therefore a theory of economic development and political economy, not merely a defense of free exchange.'],
['Theory of Moral Sentiments','Why does Smith’s moral philosophy matter for interpreting his economics?','Smith’s earlier work describes sympathy, social approval and the impartial spectator. Human motivation is plural rather than reducible to material greed. Commercial society depends on norms of trust and justice, so markets operate inside a moral and institutional order rather than replacing it.'],
['Division of Labor','Why does specialization raise productivity beyond simple repetition?','Smith identified greater dexterity, reduced switching time and specialized machinery. Modern analysis adds matching, learning-by-doing and supplier specialization. The same mechanism also creates dependence on coordination and market size: specialization pays only when enough demand exists to support narrow tasks.'],
['Extent of the Market','Why does market size affect the feasible division of labor?','A highly specialized producer needs enough buyers to cover fixed costs and sustain a narrow occupation. Larger markets therefore support more specialization, intermediate suppliers and scale economies. This creates a cumulative mechanism: market expansion enables specialization, which raises productivity and can expand markets further.'],
['Natural Price and Market Price','What is the difference between Smith’s market price and natural price?','Market price responds to temporary scarcity and demand, while the natural price reflects longer-run remuneration of wages, profits and rent needed to sustain production. Competition tends to move resources toward activities with unusually high returns and away from low-return uses, creating an adjustment process rather than a static equilibrium equation.'],
['Invisible Hand','What does the invisible-hand metaphor actually establish?','It illustrates how decentralized actions can produce unintended coordination under particular institutional conditions. It does not prove that every pursuit of self-interest maximizes welfare. Monopoly, external effects, public goods and political privilege can all break the alignment between private incentives and broader social outcomes.'],
['Self-Interest and Exchange','Why is Smith’s butcher-brewer-baker argument not a theory of universal selfishness?','Smith argues that exchange often works by appealing to the interests of counterparties rather than relying on benevolence. That is a claim about coordination, not a complete psychology. His moral philosophy explicitly recognizes sympathy, norms and the desire for social approval.'],
['Competition','How does Smith understand competition as a process rather than a market structure?','Competition reallocates capital and labor toward uses offering higher returns, pressures prices toward longer-run costs and disciplines merchants. Smith also worried that producers have strong incentives to collude. Competition therefore requires institutions that prevent private privilege from replacing market rivalry.'],
['Merchant Interests','Why was Smith suspicious of policy advice from merchants?','Merchants often possess valuable information, but they also have concentrated interests in protection, monopoly and regulation that raises their own rents. Smith’s warning anticipates modern political economy: expertise and self-interest coexist, so policy should distinguish genuine public benefit from organized private advantage.'],
['Monopoly and Collusion','Why does Smith treat monopoly as more than a pricing problem?','Monopoly raises prices and restricts competition, but it also changes political incentives by concentrating wealth and influence. Smith repeatedly criticized exclusive trading privileges and producer collusion because they could turn state policy into a mechanism for protecting rents rather than expanding general prosperity.'],
['Wages','How does Smith connect wage growth to capital accumulation?','When accumulation expands the demand for labor faster than labor supply, workers gain bargaining power and wages can rise. Smith therefore did not assume wages mechanically sit at subsistence. He linked labor conditions to the pace of economic expansion, demographic forces and institutions.'],
['Profits','What role do profits play in Smith’s growth process?','Profits reward the advance of capital and help finance further accumulation. Competition tends to compress unusually high returns, while expanding markets create new opportunities. Smith expected profit rates to respond to the abundance of capital and the intensity of competition rather than to remain fixed.'],
['Rent','Why is land rent analytically different from wages and profits?','Rent can arise because land is scarce and locations differ in productivity or access. Owners may receive income from a resource whose supply is not created by their current effort. Classical economists used rent to study how growth changes the distribution of income among social classes.'],
['Capital Accumulation','Why is saving productive in Smith only when it finances accumulation?','Smith links saving to the employment of labor and expansion of productive capital. Accumulation increases the stock of tools, inventories and productive capacity that support specialization. The mechanism is not moral praise of thrift in itself; what matters is how resources are redirected toward future production.'],
['Productive and Unproductive Labor','What was Smith trying to distinguish with productive and unproductive labor?','Smith classified labor partly by whether its output became a vendible commodity that could be accumulated as capital. The distinction does not map cleanly onto modern service economies and should not be read as saying services are useless. It reflects an eighteenth-century attempt to identify activities that expand reproducible wealth.'],
['Public Works','Why can Smith support public infrastructure without abandoning market coordination?','Infrastructure such as roads and bridges can expand markets and productivity while yielding returns too diffuse for private provision. Smith therefore saw public works as complements to commerce. He also considered user charges when benefits could be tied to users, showing concern with both provision and incentives.'],
['Education','Why did Smith see education as an economic and civic issue?','Extreme specialization can narrow workers’ capacities when tasks become repetitive. Smith therefore supported public involvement in basic education partly to counter the social effects of the division of labor. Productivity gains and human development were not treated as automatically aligned.'],
['Role of the State','What functions does Smith assign to government?','His core list includes defense, administration of justice and public works or institutions that benefit society but may not repay private providers. The framework is not laissez-faire in the sense of no state; secure rights, competition and infrastructure require public institutions.'],
['Smith Against Mercantilism','What is Smith’s central objection to mercantilist reasoning?','National wealth is the capacity to produce and consume goods, not the accumulation of money or a permanent trade surplus. Imports can increase real income when they allow resources to move toward activities with lower opportunity cost. Smith also attacked the producer privileges that mercantilist restrictions often created.'],
['Absolute Advantage','How does absolute advantage differ from comparative advantage?','Absolute advantage asks who uses fewer resources to produce a good. Smith used productivity differences to motivate specialization, but Ricardo later showed that trade can still be beneficial even when one country is absolutely more productive in every sector. Relative opportunity costs determine comparative advantage.'],
['Smithian Growth','What makes Smithian growth different from growth driven purely by more capital?','Smith emphasizes productivity gains generated by specialization, larger markets and improved organization. Market expansion can therefore create increasing returns through a deeper division of labor. Modern economic geography and endogenous-growth models revive this mechanism in more formal language.'],
['Worker Deskilling','Why can division of labor raise output while damaging worker capabilities?','Repeatedly performing a narrow task can increase productivity while reducing variety, autonomy and opportunities to develop broader skills. Smith explicitly worried about this tension. It shows that aggregate productive efficiency and the quality of work are distinct dimensions of economic organization.'],
['Commercial Society','What changes when exchange becomes the dominant mode of social coordination?','Commercial society increases interdependence among strangers because individuals specialize and obtain most necessities through exchange. This expands productive possibilities but makes institutions of contract, trust, money and justice more important. Smith viewed commerce as both an economic system and a form of social organization.'],
['Moral Norms and Markets','Why can markets depend on norms that prices themselves do not create?','Contracts are easier to sustain when honesty, trust and expectations of fair dealing reduce monitoring and enforcement costs. Smith’s moral theory helps explain why commercial exchange requires social norms alongside formal law. Markets can also reshape those norms, so the relationship runs both ways.'],
['Institutional Preconditions of Markets','What has to exist before decentralized exchange can work reliably?','Property claims, contract enforcement, predictable rules, money, physical security and limits on coercive privilege are all institutional inputs into market exchange. Smith’s system therefore treats functioning markets as politically and legally constructed environments rather than spontaneous barter operating in a vacuum.'],
['Natural Liberty','What does Smith mean by a system of natural liberty?','Individuals are generally free to pursue occupations and exchange provided they respect justice. The phrase is not a claim that all regulation is illegitimate; Smith accepted public works, education, banking restrictions in some circumstances and measures needed to sustain competition.'],
['Banking in Smith','Why did Smith support some constraints on banking despite favoring economic liberty?','Smith recognized that bank liabilities and speculative credit can create systemic effects beyond individual contracts. He supported restrictions on certain small-denomination notes and compared some financial regulations to building codes: limits on private freedom can be justified when private actions create substantial public risk.'],
['Colonies and Empire','Why was Smith critical of colonial monopoly even while analyzing imperial trade?','He argued that exclusive colonial systems benefited protected merchants while imposing military and fiscal costs on the wider society. His analysis separates the private profitability of imperial privilege from its social profitability, an early example of incidence and rent-seeking reasoning.']
]),
...g('Ricardo, Malthus & Later Classical Economics',[
['David Ricardo','Why is Ricardo fundamentally a theorist of distribution as well as trade?','Ricardo asked how economic growth divides output among wages, profits and rents. Diminishing returns on land could raise food costs and rents, squeeze profits and slow accumulation. Comparative advantage sits inside this broader concern with how scarcity and distribution shape long-run development.'],
['Comparative Advantage','Why can both countries gain from trade even if one is more productive in every sector?','What matters is relative opportunity cost. The more productive country benefits by specializing more in the activity where its productivity advantage is greatest, while the other specializes where its relative disadvantage is smallest. Trade then allows both to consume beyond their autarky possibilities, although distributional losses can remain.'],
['Ricardian Rent','Why does rent rise when cultivation expands onto worse land?','If land differs in productivity, the market price must cover production on the marginal plot needed to meet demand. More productive plots then earn a differential surplus. Rent is therefore determined by scarcity and productivity differences rather than being a cost that independently determines the crop price.'],
['Diminishing Returns to Land','Why did diminishing agricultural returns worry Ricardo?','As population and demand expand, cultivation may move to less fertile land or use existing land more intensively. Rising food costs can increase rents and put pressure on real wages and profits. In Ricardo’s model this mechanism can slow capital accumulation and push the economy toward a stationary state.'],
['Wages, Profits and Rents','Why are the classical distributive shares linked rather than independent?','Given output and technology, changes in the share going to one class affect what remains for others. Ricardo emphasized conflict between rising land rents and capitalist profits as cultivation expands. The framework treats distribution as a structural consequence of growth and scarcity, not merely as separate market prices.'],
['Ricardo on Wages','Why does Ricardo distinguish market wages from a longer-run natural wage?','Market wages can move with labor demand, but Ricardo expected demographic responses and subsistence norms to influence a longer-run level. The theory reflects nineteenth-century population assumptions and should not be treated as a modern empirical wage law.'],
['Ricardo on Profits','Why do profits tend to fall in Ricardo’s long-run model?','If food becomes more expensive because agriculture faces diminishing returns, wages must rise enough to maintain workers’ consumption standard. With output prices constrained by competition, a larger wage share compresses profits. Trade in cheaper food can postpone this mechanism.'],
['Stationary State','What drives the classical economy toward a stationary state?','Accumulation expands output and population, but scarce land generates rising rents and food costs. If profits fall enough, the incentive to accumulate capital weakens. The stationary state is thus an endpoint produced by resource constraints and distribution, not by a failure of entrepreneurship.'],
['Corn Laws','Why were the Corn Laws central to Ricardo’s political economy?','Restrictions on imported grain kept domestic food prices higher, benefiting landowners through rent while increasing wage costs for employers. Ricardo supported repeal because cheaper grain could reduce distributive pressure on profits and prolong capital accumulation.'],
['Labor Theory of Value in Ricardo','What was Ricardo trying to explain with labor embodied in commodities?','He sought a theory of relative value that was not circularly dependent on distribution. Labor requirements provided an approximate anchor, but differences in capital intensity and production time complicated the relationship. Ricardo knew the theory was imperfect and did not treat observed prices as simple labor-hour ratios.'],
['Thomas Malthus','Why is Malthus important for more than population pessimism?','Malthus developed a population theory linking income and demographic response, but he also questioned whether saving automatically generated equivalent investment demand. His arguments about general gluts and insufficient demand foreshadowed later macroeconomic disputes.'],
['Malthusian Population Theory','What mechanism keeps living standards near subsistence in the Malthusian model?','Higher income raises population through higher fertility or lower mortality; population growth then presses against relatively fixed land and resources, reducing income per person. Technological gains mainly support more people rather than permanently higher living standards until the demographic regime changes.'],
['Malthusian Trap','Why could centuries of technological improvement coexist with little sustained growth in income per person?','In a Malthusian economy, productivity gains raise population, which eventually dilutes land and other fixed resources. Total output increases, but average living standards revert toward a demographic equilibrium. The Industrial Revolution broke this pattern because technological progress accelerated while fertility behavior later changed.'],
['Preventive and Positive Checks','How does Malthus distinguish mechanisms that restrain population?','Preventive checks reduce births through delayed marriage or restraint, while positive checks raise mortality through famine, disease or conflict. Later demographic transitions showed that fertility can fall systematically with development, making the original mechanism much less deterministic than Malthus expected.'],
['Effective Demand in Malthus','Why did Malthus doubt that production automatically creates sufficient demand?','He argued that high saving could exceed profitable investment opportunities, producing deficient expenditure on output. Ricardo and Say were more confident that price and interest-rate adjustment would coordinate saving and investment. The dispute anticipates later Keynesian debates over aggregate demand.'],
['General Gluts Debate','Can an economy suffer excess supply in many markets at once?','Classical economists disagreed. Say and Ricardo emphasized that production generates income and that relative-price adjustment reallocates demand, while Malthus and Sismondi allowed broader demand deficiency. The debate is an early version of the macroeconomic question of whether aggregate spending can constrain output.'],
['Jean-Baptiste Say','What did Say actually mean by the claim later summarized as “supply creates its own demand”?','Say emphasized that producing goods generates incomes that can purchase other goods and that money is an intermediary rather than the ultimate object of exchange. Strong versions denying any aggregate-demand problem go beyond the nuance of his writings.'],
["Say's Law",'What assumption turns Say’s Law into a strong claim about aggregate demand?','If income not consumed is reliably transformed into investment through flexible interest rates and markets clear, aggregate demand need not constrain output persistently. Keynes attacked exactly this coordination assumption, arguing that desired saving and desired investment can diverge.'],
['John Stuart Mill','How did Mill extend classical economics while softening some of its claims?','Mill synthesized classical production and distribution theory, defended competition and trade, and treated many distributional institutions as socially alterable rather than technologically fixed. He also entertained a stationary state without assuming it would be socially undesirable.'],
['Production vs Distribution in Mill','Why does Mill say the laws of production and distribution differ?','Physical production is constrained by technology and resources, while the distribution of output depends much more on laws, customs and institutions. The distinction helped open classical political economy toward questions of reform without rejecting economic scarcity.'],
["Mill's Stationary State",'Why could Mill regard a stationary economy more positively than Ricardo?','Mill argued that endless accumulation and population growth were not synonymous with human improvement. Once material abundance was sufficient, a stationary economy could potentially support leisure, culture and a less competitive social order.'],
['Sismondi','Why did Sismondi challenge optimistic versions of classical accumulation?','Sismondi emphasized instability, worker insecurity and the possibility that rapid mechanization and unequal income distribution could produce insufficient demand. He is often treated as an early critic of laissez-faire political economy and a precursor to later crisis theories.'],
['Classical Growth Theory','What drives growth in the classical framework?','Capital accumulation, labor-force growth, specialization and resource constraints interact over time. Unlike later steady-state models, classical writers focused strongly on how growth changes wages, profits and rents and on the possibility that distribution itself feeds back into accumulation.'],
['Classical Theory of Distribution','Why did classical economists organize distribution around social classes?','Landowners, workers and capitalists receive rents, wages and profits through different economic mechanisms. The framework highlights functional income shares and the structural positions attached to ownership of land, labor power and capital rather than starting from individual marginal products.'],
['Classical Theory of Value','Why is classical value theory difficult to map directly onto modern price theory?','Classical writers distinguished temporary market prices from deeper determinants of normal relative prices, often emphasizing labor, production costs and distribution. Marginalist theory later made preferences and scarcity central. The two frameworks organize the problem of value differently rather than merely using different formulas.'],
['International Trade and Distribution','Why can trade alter domestic distribution even when it raises aggregate real income?','Imports change relative prices and therefore returns to land, labor and capital. Ricardo’s grain-trade analysis already linked trade policy to class distribution. Modern trade models formalize the same point through factor prices, firm heterogeneity and regional adjustment.'],
['Ricardian Equivalence Origins','What is the historical connection between Ricardo and the idea later called Ricardian equivalence?','Ricardo recognized that debt-financed spending may imply future taxation and therefore need not be perceived as net wealth. He did not believe the public would fully behave this way in practice. Modern Ricardian-equivalence propositions add strong assumptions about foresight, credit access and intergenerational links.'],
['Comparative Advantage and Wages','Why does comparative advantage not imply equal wages across countries?','Trade patterns depend on relative productivity, while wage levels depend on absolute productivity and equilibrium conditions across sectors. A highly productive country can pay higher wages and still export goods in which its relative productivity advantage is greatest.'],
['Classical Political Economy','What makes classical economics “political economy” rather than just price theory?','Classical writers studied production, distribution, institutions, taxation, trade and social classes as a connected historical system. Economic analysis was inseparable from questions about landownership, state policy and long-run development.']
]),
...g('Marx & Marxian Economics',[
['Karl Marx','What does Marx change by treating capitalism as a historically specific system rather than a timeless market economy?','Marx centers wage labor, private ownership of productive assets, commodity production and competitive accumulation. Prices matter, but the deeper analytical objects are class relations, surplus appropriation and the reproduction of the system over time.'],
['Historical Materialism','How can material production shape political and social institutions without mechanically determining them?','Historical materialism emphasizes how modes of production structure interests, class relations and institutional possibilities. Strong deterministic readings oversimplify Marx. The useful version is relational: economic organization constrains and interacts with politics, law and ideology rather than dictating every outcome.'],
['Commodity','Why is a commodity more than simply a good sold for money in Marx’s analysis?','A commodity combines use value with exchange value and is produced for exchange. The commodity form matters because social coordination among separate producers occurs indirectly through markets, so relationships among people appear through relationships among things and prices.'],
['Use Value','Why is use value necessary but insufficient for explaining exchange?','A commodity must satisfy some want to be saleable, but usefulness alone does not determine its exchange relation. Marx uses the distinction to separate the qualitative usefulness of goods from the social form through which they acquire value in commodity production.'],
['Exchange Value','Why does Marx treat exchange value as a social relation rather than an intrinsic property?','Exchange value expresses quantitative relations among commodities in exchange. Marx’s analysis searches for the social conditions that make heterogeneous goods commensurable, leading him toward socially necessary labor time rather than treating exchange ratios as natural properties of objects.'],
['Commodity Fetishism','What exactly is “fetishized” in commodity exchange?','Social relations among producers appear as objective relations among commodities and prices. The concept is not mainly about consumer obsession. It describes how market coordination hides the underlying social organization of labor behind apparently impersonal exchange relations.'],
['Labor Power','Why is labor power distinct from labor in Marx’s theory?','Workers sell their capacity to work for a period, not a finished quantity of labor embodied in advance. Once employed, that capacity can generate more value during the working day than the value represented by the wage. This distinction is central to Marx’s account of surplus value.'],
['Socially Necessary Labor Time','Why does Marx use socially necessary rather than individual labor time?','A producer cannot create more value simply by working inefficiently. Value is related to the labor time required under normal conditions with prevailing techniques and intensity. Competition therefore disciplines producers toward socially prevailing productivity.'],
["Marx's Value Theory",'Why should Marx’s value theory not be reduced to the statement “prices equal labor hours”?','Marx distinguishes values, market prices and prices of production. Competition reallocates surplus across sectors with different capital structures, so observed prices need not equal embodied labor values. The theory is intended to explain social production and surplus, not to provide a one-line price formula.'],
['Surplus Value','Where does surplus value arise in Marx’s account if commodities exchange at their values?','The distinctive commodity labor power can produce more value during the working day than is required to reproduce its own value through wages. The difference becomes surplus value. The argument locates exploitation in production rather than requiring unequal exchange.'],
['Absolute Surplus Value','How can surplus rise without any productivity improvement?','Absolute surplus value increases when the working day is lengthened or work is intensified while necessary labor time remains broadly unchanged. It is the direct route to increasing the share of labor time performed beyond that required to reproduce wages.'],
['Relative Surplus Value','How can productivity growth increase surplus value even if the working day does not lengthen?','If productivity lowers the labor required to produce wage goods, the socially necessary labor time needed to reproduce labor power can fall. A larger portion of a fixed working day then becomes surplus labor.'],
['Exploitation','What does exploitation mean in Marxian economics rather than ordinary moral language?','It refers to a structural relation in which workers produce surplus value controlled by owners of capital. The concept depends on Marx’s value theory and ownership structure; it is not identical to low wages, mistreatment or illegal conduct.'],
['Constant Capital','Why does Marx distinguish machines and materials from wages?','Constant capital transfers existing value to output through depreciation or material use but, in Marx’s framework, does not itself create new value. The category contrasts with variable capital spent on labor power, which is treated as the source of new value and surplus.'],
['Variable Capital','Why is wage expenditure called variable capital?','Capital advanced for labor power can return as a larger value because workers create more value than the equivalent of their wages. The “variable” refers to this capacity for value expansion within Marx’s theoretical system.'],
['Organic Composition of Capital','Why does rising capital intensity matter for Marx’s long-run theory?','The organic composition captures the relationship between capital invested in means of production and labor power, interpreted through value categories. Marx expected technological competition to raise it, creating implications for employment and profitability that are central to his accumulation theory.'],
['Capital Accumulation','Why is reinvestment not optional for the capitalist firm in Marx’s competitive framework?','Competition pressures firms to expand, mechanize and reduce costs. Accumulation is therefore not merely personal greed but a systemic imperative: firms that fail to reinvest risk losing market position to rivals that do.'],
['Simple Reproduction','What does simple reproduction reveal about capitalism even without growth?','Simple reproduction assumes surplus is consumed rather than accumulated and production repeats at the same scale. Marx uses it to show how class relations and ownership are reproduced through recurring flows of wages, consumption and replacement of capital.'],
['Expanded Reproduction','What changes when surplus is reinvested rather than consumed?','Part of surplus becomes additional capital, expanding future production. This generates growth but also changes the scale and structure of demand across sectors. Marx’s reproduction schemes study whether the departments producing consumption and investment goods can remain mutually consistent.'],
['Reserve Army of Labor','How can unemployment affect accumulation even when it represents unused labor?','A pool of unemployed or precariously employed workers can restrain wage pressure and provide labor for expansions. Marx therefore treats unemployment partly as endogenous to accumulation and technological change rather than solely as an accidental deviation from full employment.'],
['Primitive Accumulation','Why does Marx begin capitalism with a history of dispossession rather than voluntary saving?','Primitive accumulation describes processes that separated producers from direct access to land and other means of subsistence while concentrating property. Enclosure, colonial extraction and state power helped create both wage workers and owners of capital.'],
['Alienation','How does ownership and control of production enter Marx’s theory of alienation?','Workers can become estranged from the product, the labor process, other people and their own capacities when work is organized under objectives they do not control. The concept links economic structure to lived experience rather than merely job dissatisfaction.'],
['Class Relations','Why are classes defined by production relations rather than income brackets alone?','For Marx, the key distinction concerns control over productive assets and the need to sell labor power. Income matters, but class position is rooted in how individuals participate in production and appropriate surplus.'],
['Class Struggle','Why does common economic position not automatically generate collective action?','Shared interests create potential conflict over wages, control and surplus, but workers and owners also compete among themselves. Organization, institutions and political conditions determine whether class interests become coordinated collective action.'],
['Concentration of Capital','What is the difference between concentration and centralization of capital?','Concentration occurs when firms grow by accumulating their own surplus. Centralization occurs when existing capitals combine through mergers, acquisitions or failures. Both can increase scale, but the mechanisms are different.'],
['Tendency of the Rate of Profit to Fall','Why is Marx’s falling-profit argument a tendency rather than a simple prediction of permanent decline?','If capital intensity rises while surplus originates in labor, profitability faces downward pressure. Marx also lists counterforces: higher exploitation, cheaper capital goods, faster turnover, devaluation and foreign trade. Empirical testing is difficult because theoretical value categories do not map cleanly onto national accounts.'],
['Counteracting Tendencies','Why are counteracting forces essential to understanding Marx’s profit-rate theory?','They explain why a structural pressure need not appear as a monotonic observed decline. Firms can raise exploitation, cheapen machinery, reorganize production or devalue capital during crises. The theory therefore predicts conflict among forces rather than a one-direction time series.'],
['Transformation Problem','Why is the transformation from values to prices a major issue in Marxian theory?','Industries use different ratios of labor to capital but competitive capital tends to seek comparable profit rates. Marx therefore needs a mapping from labor-value categories to prices of production that redistributes surplus across sectors. The consistency of that transformation has generated a large technical literature.'],
['Prices of Production','Why can prices of production diverge from labor values while preserving Marx’s distributional argument?','Prices of production incorporate cost prices plus an average profit rate. Competition moves capital across industries, equalizing profitability and redistributing surplus generated across the system. They are an intermediate theoretical construct between value categories and fluctuating market prices.'],
['Marxian Crisis Theory','Why is there no single Marxian theory of crisis?','Different interpretations emphasize profitability, overaccumulation, realization problems, sectoral disproportions, credit expansion or financial fragility. A useful Marxian crisis explanation must identify which mechanism is binding in the episode rather than invoking contradiction as a catch-all cause.']
]),
...g('Marginal Revolution & Neoclassical Economics',[
['Marginal Revolution','What changed when economists began explaining choice at the margin?','Jevons, Menger and Walras shifted value theory toward incremental tradeoffs among scarce alternatives. The marginal unit, not total usefulness, became central. This move laid the foundation for modern consumer choice, firm optimization and equilibrium analysis.'],
['William Stanley Jevons','What was Jevons’s contribution to the marginal revolution?','Jevons formalized utility-based choice and emphasized that exchange depends on marginal utility rather than total utility. His mathematical approach helped move economics toward explicit optimization, although his psychology and cardinal-utility assumptions were later revised.'],
['Carl Menger','How did Menger’s version of marginalism differ from Walrasian formalism?','Menger emphasized subjective value, causal processes and the ranking of wants rather than simultaneous mathematical equilibrium. His approach became foundational for the Austrian tradition and its focus on market processes and heterogeneous knowledge.'],
['Léon Walras','Why is Walras central to general equilibrium theory?','Walras modeled many markets simultaneously, with prices adjusting so supply equals demand across the system. The framework made interdependence explicit: changing one market can affect all others through income, substitution and resource constraints.'],
['Marginal Utility','Why does marginal rather than total utility help explain price?','The value of one additional unit depends on the least urgent use it serves at the margin. Water can have enormous total usefulness yet low marginal value where abundant, while scarce diamonds can have high marginal value.'],
['Diminishing Marginal Utility','What does diminishing marginal utility actually assume?','Holding other things constant, additional units of a good contribute smaller increments to utility as consumption rises. In modern ordinal theory, economists often avoid treating utility increments as directly measurable and instead work with preference curvature and substitution.'],
['Subjective Value','How can value be subjective without prices being arbitrary?','Individual willingness to pay reflects preferences and circumstances, while market prices emerge from interaction among many valuations, resource constraints and technologies. Subjectivity concerns the source of valuation, not the absence of objective scarcity or market discipline.'],
['Marginal Productivity','How does marginal productivity enter factor demand?','A profit-maximizing competitive firm hires an input until the value of its marginal product equals its price, subject to assumptions about technology and market structure. This becomes a theory of factor demand, not automatically a complete theory of income distribution.'],
['Alfred Marshall','Why is Marshall’s partial-equilibrium method still useful?','Marshall isolates one market while treating many other variables as fixed, making comparative statics manageable. He also distinguished market, short-run and long-run adjustment, showing that supply responses and incidence depend strongly on time horizon.'],
['Partial Equilibrium','When does analyzing one market in isolation become misleading?','Partial equilibrium works when feedback through income, input markets and related goods is small. It becomes unreliable when a policy is large, a market is central or many prices adjust together. General equilibrium then becomes necessary.'],
['Supply and Demand','Why are supply and demand not directly observable curves in market data?','Observed prices and quantities are equilibrium outcomes of both schedules simultaneously. Identifying one curve requires exogenous variation that shifts the other or another credible source of independent movement. This is a core econometric identification problem.'],
['Elasticity','Why is elasticity more useful than a simple slope?','Elasticity measures proportional responsiveness and is independent of measurement units. It allows comparison across goods and markets and determines incidence, revenue responses and the size of quantity adjustments to shocks.'],
['Consumer Surplus','What assumptions make consumer surplus a meaningful welfare measure?','Consumer surplus approximates the difference between willingness to pay and actual expenditure. It is most straightforward when income effects are small. For large changes, compensating or equivalent variation provides a more theoretically precise money-metric measure.'],
['Producer Surplus','Why is producer surplus not identical to profit?','Producer surplus subtracts variable opportunity costs from revenue, whereas economic profit also accounts for fixed and capital opportunity costs. In the short run a firm can earn positive producer surplus while failing to cover total economic cost.'],
['Short Run vs Long Run','Why can the same shock have very different elasticities over different horizons?','In the short run, capital, contracts and habits may be fixed. Over longer horizons firms can enter, consumers can substitute, capacity can change and technology can adapt. Incidence and welfare analysis therefore depend on the time available for adjustment.'],
['Representative Firm','What analytical role did Marshall’s representative firm play?','It was an attempt to reason about an industry in which firms differ in age, scale and efficiency while normal competitive conditions prevail. The concept became controversial because aggregation and increasing returns create difficulties for a single representative producer.'],
['Vilfredo Pareto','What did Pareto change in utility and welfare analysis?','Pareto helped move consumer theory toward ordinal preferences and introduced the efficiency criterion now bearing his name. This allowed economics to analyze feasible improvements without requiring interpersonal comparison of utility levels.'],
['Pareto Efficiency','Why can a Pareto-efficient allocation still be extremely unequal?','Pareto efficiency only means no one can be made better off without making someone else worse off. It says nothing about fairness, initial endowments or distribution. Efficiency and equity are separate normative dimensions.'],
['Edgeworth Box','What does an Edgeworth box reveal that a single-market diagram cannot?','It displays all feasible allocations of two goods between two people and shows where their indifference curves are tangent. The contract curve traces Pareto-efficient allocations, making exchange efficiency and distribution visually distinct.'],
['Arthur Pigou','Why is Pigou central to welfare economics?','Pigou analyzed divergences between private and social costs or benefits. Corrective taxes or subsidies can align private decisions with social marginal effects when externalities prevent market prices from doing so.'],
['Pigouvian Tax','What must be measured correctly for a Pigouvian tax to be efficient?','The tax should equal marginal external damage at the efficient quantity, not average harm. Heterogeneity, uncertainty, monitoring and distribution can make implementation much harder than the textbook rule suggests.'],
['Externalities','Why are externalities a failure of the price system rather than merely “bad side effects”?','An externality affects another party through a channel not incorporated into the decision-maker’s marginal incentives. The resulting private and social costs diverge, so decentralized choices need not maximize total surplus.'],
['Neoclassical Consumer','What is the core structure of the neoclassical consumer model?','Preferences rank feasible consumption bundles and a budget constraint limits choice. Demand emerges from maximizing preferences subject to that constraint. The model is flexible about motives but demanding about consistency and information in its strongest forms.'],
['Neoclassical Firm','What is the core structure of the neoclassical firm model?','A firm chooses inputs and outputs to maximize profit given technology and prices. Under perfect competition it takes prices as given; under market power it must also account for how its decisions affect prices or demand.'],
['Constrained Optimization','Why is the constraint often more economically informative than the objective?','Many agents would prefer more of desirable outcomes; scarcity enters through budgets, technology, time and institutional restrictions. Shadow values reveal how much relaxing a binding constraint would improve the objective.'],
['Lagrange Multiplier','What is the economic meaning of a Lagrange multiplier?','At an optimum, the multiplier measures the marginal value of relaxing a constraint. It can therefore be interpreted as a shadow price on scarce resources such as time, capacity, emissions or capital.'],
['First Welfare Theorem','What does the first welfare theorem actually require?','Under conditions including competitive price-taking, complete markets and no relevant externalities, a competitive equilibrium is Pareto efficient. The theorem is conditional; it provides a benchmark for diagnosing which frictions break efficiency.'],
['Second Welfare Theorem','Why does the second welfare theorem separate efficiency from distribution in theory?','Under convexity and other assumptions, any Pareto-efficient allocation can be supported as a competitive equilibrium after suitable lump-sum redistribution of initial resources. In practice, lump-sum redistribution is difficult, so efficiency and distribution cannot usually be separated cleanly.'],
['General Equilibrium','Why is general equilibrium more than “many supply-and-demand graphs at once”?','Prices, incomes and resource use are jointly determined across markets. A shock in one sector changes purchasing power, input demand and substitution elsewhere. General equilibrium forces counterfactual analysis to respect these system-wide consistency conditions.'],
['Limits of Competitive Equilibrium','Which assumptions most often break the clean welfare result?','Market power, externalities, asymmetric information, missing markets, nonconvexities and incomplete contracts can all create wedges between decentralized equilibrium and efficient allocation. Modern microeconomics largely studies these departures rather than assuming them away.']
])
];

